Teach maspsx that a sized .extern can be small data.

cc1 states the size of an extern whose type it knows: `.extern D_8005E44C, 2`.
GNU as places such a symbol in small data when the size is within -G, which
makes it gp-relative -- a single instruction, leaving the load delay exposed.
maspsx skipped .extern lines entirely, so _uses_gp could not see this and
assumed the following instruction would need an $at expansion to reach its
symbol. That expansion separates the load from its use, so maspsx dropped the
`#nop` cc1 had placed in the delay slot, and the function came out one
instruction shorter than retail.

The externs are kept in their own dict rather than added to sbss_entries,
because sbss_entries is also used to emit `.section .sbss` and allocate
storage, and to rewrite stores into explicit %gp_rel. An extern must do
neither: this unit does not define the symbol. The new dict is read only by
_uses_gp, so this changes exactly one decision -- whether a load-delay nop is
required -- and can emit nothing.

Applied with `git apply --recount`, so the hunk line counts below are not
authoritative; the context lines are.

--- a/maspsx/__init__.py
+++ b/maspsx/__init__.py
@@ -473,6 +473,9 @@
         self.bss_entries: dict[str, int] = {}
         self.sbss_entries: dict[str, int] = {}
         self.sdata_entries: dict[str, int] = {}
+        # Externs whose stated size is within the sdata limit. Read only by
+        # _uses_gp; never emitted, because this unit does not define them.
+        self.sextern_entries: dict[str, int] = {}

         self.comm_symbols: set[str] = set()

@@ -519,6 +522,17 @@
             if line.startswith(".extern"):
                 in_sdata = False
+                # e.g. `.extern D_8005E44C, 2`. cc1 states the size when it
+                # knows the type; GNU as then makes the symbol small data if
+                # the size is within -G, and reaches it through $gp in a
+                # single instruction, leaving the load delay exposed.
+                rest = line[len(".extern"):].strip()
+                if "," in rest:
+                    symbol, _, size_str = rest.partition(",")
+                    symbol = symbol.strip()
+                    size_str = size_str.strip()
+                    if symbol and size_str.isdigit():
+                        if 0 < int(size_str) <= self.sdata_limit:
+                            self.sextern_entries[symbol] = int(size_str)
                 continue

             if line.startswith(".comm") or line.startswith(".lcomm"):
@@ -580,6 +594,7 @@
         self.bss_entries = {}
         self.sbss_entries = {}
         self.sdata_entries = {}
+        self.sextern_entries = {}

         self.preprocess_lines()

@@ -687,7 +702,9 @@
                 if gp_allowed and (
-                    symbol in self.sbss_entries or symbol in self.sdata_entries
+                    symbol in self.sbss_entries
+                    or symbol in self.sdata_entries
+                    or symbol in self.sextern_entries
                 ):
                     return True
